t do make money via those strategies, but they are a minority. 7. Conclusion: the monetization strategies are aspirational, and user experience reflects reality. Make it conversational. No markdown except simple formatting if needed. I'll use plain text with occasional bold/italics maybe. I'll incorporate user's word "abandoned". I'll craft the response.
15:16:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right—and honestly, you’ve put your finger on the dirty little secret of the app stores. My earlier answer describes how free apps *can theoretically* make money, but the reality you’re seeing is far more common: most free apps never make real money at all, and that’s exactly why they feel abandoned. A few things are going on under the hood: **1. The long tail of “hobby projects” and learning exercises** A massive chunk of free apps are built by solo developers who made something for fun, for their portfolio, or to solve their own problem. They never intended to build a business. They might drop a few updates in the first months, then life gets in the way, and the app just sits there. It’s not abandoned with malice—it’s just a side project that ran out of steam. **2. The math is brutal unless you hit critical mass** Even if an app does show ads, the revenue per user is tiny. If you have 1,000 loyal users, you might earn $1–$5 *per month* from banner ads. That’s not enough to pay for a coffee, let alone motivate serious updates. Most free apps simply never get the hundreds of thousands of downloads needed to make advertising viable. So the ads you do see might be an afterthought, generating pocket change that doesn’t justify more work. **3. “Set it and forget it” zombie apps** Some developers cobble together an app, put in a minimal ad SDK, and upload it to the store. If the app still works and the server costs are negligible, they just leave it running. They might log in once a year to collect a $20 AdSense check. No updates needed because the app isn’t broken, just frozen in time. That’s the “abandoned” feel you’re picking up on. **4. Failed monetization attempts** The creator might have tried a freemium model, but nobody bought the premium version. Or they launched with a subscription, saw zero conversions, and gave up. Without revenue, there’s zero incentive to keep polishing. The app becomes a ghost of what they once dreamed. **5. The sheer size of the graveyard** When you browse app stores, you’re wading through millions of apps. The top 1% of free apps (the ones that do make serious money) are built by teams with marketing budgets and proper monetization strategies. The other 99% are passion projects, student experiments, or half‑finished prototypes that were never meant to be updated for years. Statistically, every abandoned app you’ve used is totally normal. **6. “Indie” doesn’t always mean “business”** Many developers put their app out there with zero business plan. They might think, “I’ll figure out monetization later,” but later never comes. Or they slap on a donation button and get two bucks in a year. Without a clear path to revenue, the app isn’t a business—it’s a digital art project that slowly fades. So your experience is the true picture: **most free app creators aren’t making meaningful money at all.** The strategies I listed earlier are what the successful minority use. For everyone else, “free” just means they’re footing the server bills out of pocket until they eventually stop caring. It’s a bit of a paradox—there are more free apps than ever, but only a tiny fraction are actively maintained or profitable. Your gut feeling is spot on.
